WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.095
OpenAI preprint proves

00:00:01.907 --> 00:00:03.463
the matrix multiplication

00:00:03.275 --> 00:00:04.783
exponent is exactly

00:00:04.595 --> 00:00:05.585
9/4

00:00:06.102 --> 00:00:07.269
A new OpenAI

00:00:07.081 --> 00:00:08.165
preprint proves a

00:00:07.977 --> 00:00:09.262
matching lower bound

00:00:09.486 --> 00:00:10.818
any formal arithmetic

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:11.785
circuit multiplying

00:00:11.597 --> 00:00:12.611
two n ×

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:13.826
n matrices needs

00:00:13.637 --> 00:00:14.510
at least n

00:00:14.321 --> 00:00:16.160
/ 2 multiplication

00:00:15.972 --> 00:00:17.234
or division gates

00:00:17.758 --> 00:00:19.019
The 9/4 upper

00:00:18.831 --> 00:00:19.927
bound was already

00:00:19.739 --> 00:00:21.130
established over C

00:00:20.942 --> 00:00:22.026
by OpenAI

00:00:22.086 --> 00:00:23.170
and over every

00:00:22.982 --> 00:00:24.208
field by Amanov

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:25.458
van den Berg

00:00:25.364 --> 00:00:26.236
and Zuiddam

00:00:26.774 --> 00:00:28.071
The lower-bound proof

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:28.920
shows a smaller

00:00:28.732 --> 00:00:30.099
exponent would compress

00:00:29.911 --> 00:00:31.255
tensor networks enough

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:31.986
to violate a

00:00:31.798 --> 00:00:32.823
lower bound for

00:00:32.635 --> 00:00:33.967
testing whether entries

00:00:33.791 --> 00:00:35.123
in several independent

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:36.314
lists are distinct

00:00:36.846 --> 00:00:37.860
Full story on

00:00:37.671 --> 00:00:38.567
the dashboard
